# patching-hosts knob (#131): what only this repo's owner knows about patching
# co-replicator. Grammar: skills/patching-hosts/references/knob.md. Times UTC.

class production
posture scheduled

# Largely pre-production (owner, 2026-10-06): any day, all day. Narrow these
# once a consumer depends on fetch latency. No quiet range: no timer on the
# host does work a step could overlap, and the bus queues across a restart.
window Mon 00:00-23:59
window Tue 00:00-23:59
window Wed 00:00-23:59
window Thu 00:00-23:59
window Fri 00:00-23:59
window Sat 00:00-23:59
window Sun 00:00-23:59

# This worker's own unacked commands across the groups it consumes; fails
# closed. scripts/inflight.py says why it reads this consumer, not the group.
inflight cd /home/exedev/replicator && .venv/bin/python -m scripts.inflight

service replicator.service
health systemctl is-active --quiet replicator.service
# The broker round-trip: the same read exits non-zero when co-broker is unreachable.
health cd /home/exedev/replicator && .venv/bin/python -m scripts.inflight >/dev/null

# No restarter: Restart=on-failure is the unit's own, and OnFailure= only
# notifies (FAILURE-NOTIFICATION.md). No caller: producers reach this host
# through the bus, which holds their commands while the worker is down.
# No datastore and no backup: the state is on the broker and in object storage.

# Tailscale, followed and scoped by deploy/apt/preferences.d/tailscale.pref.
# Its postinst restarts tailscaled, so it is its own approved step. The worker
# orders After=tailscaled.service only, so that restart costs a tailnet blip,
# not a worker restart.
origin pkgs.tailscale.com follow
hold tailscale tailscale

records CannObserv/replicator
